Two dimensional (2D) nanomaterials with extended lateral surface and atomic thickness are attracting increasing attentions owing to their intriguing physicochemical properties.1,2 The emergence of various newly developed 2D nanomaterials such as graphene, $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ and metal oxides/hydroxides provides desirable applications for electronic devices, energy storage, semiconductors and catalysts.3-5 To obtain 2D nanomaterials with entire morphology and regular shape at their genesis, "bottom-up" strategies are usually preferred.6-8 Previous works have presented 2D nanomaterials in well-defined geometric shape such as layered double hydroxides,9,10 transition metal dichalcogenides11,12 and even metallic Pd.13,14 Stimulated by the keen interest of these high-aspectratio species, innovative architectures that can be nanosized in a specific orientation are still needed.15,16
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) exhibit imposing structural diversity constructed through varieties of transition metal species and numerous types of polytopic organic coordinating linkers.17-20 Compared to those traditional inorganic materials, MOFs provide versatile structure tunability originated from extensive connection topology, offering accessible refinement on the nanostructure of the periodic network.21-24 Considerable efforts have been devoted to the miniaturization of crystal size towards 2D MOF nanosheets known as surface modification, buffer layer precipitation and interfacial assembly.25-29 However, nanoplates featuring specific morphologies, e.g., uniform hexagons with ultrathin thickness, were rarely reported. Due to the scarceness of robust layered frameworks and ambiguous mechanism of the capping agents, it remains a challenge to develop a universal methodology correlating the self-assembly processes and their underlying topologic structure.30-32
In the present work, a topology-guided synthesis of a novel 2D nickel-based MOF hexagonal nanoplates (defined as HXP) was realized under the inhibition and modulation of pyridine via a substitution-suppression process. The correlation between topological nanostructure and crystal growth preference was demonstrated through a judicious choice of a specific (3,4)-connected $\mathrm{Ni_{2}(B D C)_{2}(D A B C O)}$ $(\mathrm{BDC}~=~1,4\$ -benzenedicar-boxylic acid, $\mathrm{DABCO}=1{,}4$ -diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane) framework with regular hexagonal/triangle channels to underlie the geometry of resultant morphology. Subsequent pyrolysis treatment converted the HXP into N-doped $\mathrm{Ni}@$ carbon nanocomposite, which could be applied as very efficient electrocatalyst in oxygen evolution reaction (OER).
The HXP was obtained via a pyridine assisted bottom-up method (Figure 1a). A pair of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ cations are coordinated with eight oxygen atoms from four BDC anions to form a paddlewheel SBU and then connected via DABCO molecules through the terminal nitrogen atoms. In the presence of pyridine, which exhibits similar molecular size to DABCO but can only provide one nitrogen atom to present as an inhibitor, the DABCO molecules on crystal surface might be partially replaced by pyridine molecules, leading to a strong tendency to grow into two dimensional morphology, as shown in Figure 1b and c. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images determine the HXP as uniform hexagonal pieces with smooth faces and clear edges. The lateral size is ranged from 600 to $800~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and the thickness is measured to be as small as $20~\mathrm{nm}$ in atomic force microscopy (AFM) image (Figure 1d), which features a high aspect ratio over 40. According to the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns as compared in Figure 1e, the HXP features the same crystal structure with that of $\mathrm{Ni_{2}(B D C)_{2}(D A B C O)\cdot(D M F)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{4}}$ reported by P. Maniam and N. Stock.33 The paddlewheel SBUs then self-assemble to construct (3,4)-connected kagome layers in $a b$ plane and linked via DABCO along $c$ axis (Figure 1f and g). It should be noticed that the conspicuous hexagonal channel shaped by 6 SBUs with a pore size of $\overset{\cdot}{14}\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}$ might be the topological template for the microcrystal development of hexagonal morphology. The porosity of the HXP was evaluated through the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherm under $77~\mathrm{K}$ (Figure S1). The isotherm can be identified as Type I with a H4 hysteresis loop at a high-pressure region over $P/P o{>}0.8$ , referring to inherent micropores in the network and slitlike mesopores constructed by the aggregation of nanoplates. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area was calculated as $1360~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The inset curve indicates uniform pore size distribution centered around $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which corresponds to the theoretical diameter of the hexagonal channel in consideration of the van der Waals surface of compassed network.
Insights into the mechanism of thickness modification were provided via the fabrication of nanodisks and nanorods with hexagonal transverse under a progressively decreased amount of pyridine. Keeping other reaction conditions identical to the nanoplates, the reduction of pyridine to 2/3 and 1/3 led to the morphology transformation into hexagonal nanodisks (defined as HXD)
with thickness over $100\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2a and b) and nanorods (defined as HXR) in the length of $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2c and $\mathbf{d}$ ) separately. Meanwhile, it could be observed that the transversal size of HXD and HXR retained in the range from 600 to $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ and their XRD patterns were also similar with HXP (Figure S2). This indicates that the layer stacking process could be precisely controlled by the amount of pyridine, but the lateral morphology governed by the structural hexagonal channel was robust enough to remain unchanged. Here the topological structure of $\mathrm{Ni_{2}(B D C)_{2}(D A B C O)}$ offers a layered structure with different combination of in-plane and out-of-plane modes. The in-plane ionic bonds between $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and BDC ions are strong enough to preclude the pyridine molecules and maintain the hexagonal channels, while the DABCO linkage in the interlayer could be substituted gradually with the increase of pyridine, presenting a substitution-suppression process and leading to the thickness reduction from nanorods to nanodisks, and finally to nanoplates. However, a total removal of pyridine resulted in completely collapsed morphology and disappearance of (100) diffraction peak in the XRD pattern (Figure S3 and S4). H. Chun and J. Moon have reported an isostructural $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ crystal with hexagonal channels supported by solvent aggregation via intermolecular hydrogen bonding between DMF and ethylene glycol molecules.34 Therefore, it is rational to speculate that excessive pyridine molecules in hexagonal geometry could also infiltrate into the cavities and act as templates for sizing the hexagonal channels through $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking.
It has been well documented that the MOFs could be converted into metal/carbon composite as precursors via thermal treatment in inert atmosphere (Figure 3a).35-38 Thermal gravity (TG) analysis of HXP indicates a major weight loss of $50\%$ occurred around $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , referring to the decomposition of organic ligands (Figure S5). The product annealed under $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 hours (defined as $\operatorname{HXP}@\operatorname{NC}800)$ was indexable to be a metallic $\mathrm{Ni}$ phase together with a broad diffraction peak caused by amorphous carbon (Figure 3b). The overall morphology of $\operatorname{HXP@NC800}$ was characterized by SEM images in Figure S6. An individual annealed nanoplate shown in Figure 3c reveals that the hexagonal morphology could be retained nearly perfect. A magnified corner clearly depicts uniformly embedded nanoparticles with average diameter around $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ in the carbon matrix (Figure S7). The high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image and selected-area electron diffraction (SEAD) pattern determine the nanoparticles as metallic Ni (Figure 3d). The lattice fringes measured at $0.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ can be indexed to the interplanar distance of the (111) planes in the cubic Ni, while the surrounding layers with a $d$ -spacing of $0.34~\mathrm{nm}$ can be ascribed to the (002) planes in the graphene-like carbon shell (Figure 3e). The ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherm of $\operatorname{HXP@NC800}$ shown in Figure S8 features a typical type IV adsorption behavior with H4 hysteresis loop, indicating the destruction of micropores with the collapse of organic skeleton. But the slit-like mesopores were retained owing to the preservation of 2D morphology. Meanwhile, the BET surface area was calculated to be $89\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is much smaller than the HXP precursor.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) results of Ni, C and possibly residual N elements in the composite are shown in Figure S9. The survey spectrum in Figure S9a exhibits intense peaks for C and Ni with bonding energy centered at $285\mathrm{eV}$ and $853~\mathrm{eV}$ , whereas the peak for $\mathbf{N}$ around $400~\mathrm{eV}$ is hardly discernible. The high-resolution spectrum of $\mathrm{Ni}~2\mathrm{p}$ features two strong peaks centered at $870.3{\ensuremath{\mathrm{~eV}}}$ and $853.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ with two accompanying satellite peaks, which can be ascribed to the Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ and Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ in metallic Ni, respectively (Figure S9b).39 The C 1s core level peak can be deconvoluted into three peaks centered at $290.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ , $286.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $284.8\ \mathrm{~eV}$ , which are assigned to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ , C-N and C-C bonds (Figure S9c).40 In the high-resolution $\mathbf{N}$ 1s spectrum (Figure S9d), the resolved peak divided into three components centered at $403.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ , 401.1 eV and $398.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be attributed to graphitic N, pyrrolic $\mathbf{N}$ and pyridinic N, respectively, which further confirms N-doping in the graphene-like carbon layers.41
The electrocatalytic performances of the annealed N-doped $\operatorname{Ni}{\textcircled{a}}$ Carbon composites for OER were evaluated in 1 M KOH electrolyte using a typical three-electrode system. The influence of thickness was examined via the comparison of $\operatorname{HXP@NC800}$ catalysts with those derived from nanodisks (defined as $\operatorname{HXD}@\operatorname{NC}800)$ ) and nanorods (defined as $\operatorname{HXR}@\operatorname{NC}800)$ . The XRD patterns of these catalysts determined the same phase shared with $\operatorname{HXP@NC800}$ (Figure S10). According to the linear sweep voltammogram (LSV) (Figure 4a) and corresponding Tafel plots (Figure 4b), the $\operatorname{HXP@NC800}$ exhibits the highest catalytic activity with an overpotential of $307~\mathrm{mV}$ at current density of 10 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and Tafel slope of $48~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , which are much lower than those of $\mathrm{HXD}@\mathrm{NC}800$ $340~\mathrm{\mV}$ , $62~\mathrm{\mV~\dec^{-1}}$ ) and HXR $\varpi\bf N C800$ ( $355~\mathrm{mV}$ , $63~\mathrm{mV}$ dec‒1). The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) depicted in Figure 4c reveals that the $\operatorname{HXP@NC800}$ features the smallest charge transfer resistance obeying a decreasing order from nanorods to nanodisks and to nanoplates, suggesting superior charge transfer dynamics resulted from the decrease of thickness. A further investigation on electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) reveals an oppositely increasing order from $\operatorname{HXR}@\operatorname{NC}800$ to $\operatorname{HXD}@\operatorname{NC}800$ and to $\operatorname{HXP@NC800}$ , suggesting more active sites might be exposed with higher aspect ratio (Figure S11). The chronoamperometry of $\operatorname{HXP@NC800}$ at an overpotential of $307\mathrm{mV}$ shown in Figure 4d exhibits satisfactory stability with a negligible decrease less than $5\%$ in 8 hours. N-doped $\mathrm{Ni}@$ carbon catalysts were also obtained under different annealing temperature (Figure S12). Their electrochemical activities were enhanced with the increase of annealing temperature from 500 to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , whereas dropped significantly at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S13). This could be attributed to the thickness evolution of the carbon shell. The thickness reduction of carbon layers resulted from raised annealing temperature can accelerate the charge transfer process. However, an excessive removal of carbon will lead to insufficient coverage of Ni core, resulting in higher charge transfer resistance and reduced catalytic activity42.
By taking advantage of the porosity of HXP precursor, the nanoplates could serve as a substrate for homogeneous physical doping of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ (Figure S14 and 15). Subsequent pyrolysis treatment of the Fe-doped HXP led to the formation of N-doped Ni/Fe@carbon electrocatalyst with well retained hexagonal nanoplates morphology (Figure  S16). The N-doped Ni/Fe $@$ carbon electrocatalyst exhibited an enhanced OER performance with a much lower overpotential of $266~\mathrm{mV}$ at current density of $10~\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which stands out as one of the best in the recently reported most advanced Ni-based electrocatalysts (Figure S17-19 and Table S1).
In summary, nickel-based MOF nanoplates with unique 2D hexagonal morphology were synthesized, in which a topological network was guided by the inhibition and modulation of pyridine. A substitution-suppression process was proposed for the mechanism of thickness modification from nanoplates to nanodisks and nanorods. Subsequent carbonization in inert gas converted the MOFs precursor into N-doped $\mathrm{Ni}@$ carbon composite, which could be applied as a very efficient electrocatalyst for OER. This work pioneered the topology-guided bottom-up synthesis toward a unique morphology, promising a new strategy to the rational design for the 2D N-doped metal alloy $@$ carbon catalysts.
